> s1(x: i8 in -8..7) = match x { -8 => 100, -7..-1 => 50, 0 => 0, 1..7 => 10 }
  note: match has no default arm: other values are outside the domain
> s1e(x: i8 in -8..7) = x < -7 ? 100 : (x < 0 ? 50 : (x == 0 ? 0 : 10))
> equiv s1 ~ s1e
s1 and s1e are equal on the domain of s1 (exhaustive)
> range s1
range of s1 -> int:
  0..100
  fits in u7
  over all 16 points of the domain
> domain s1
domain of s1:
  x: i8, -8..7
  requires an arm of match/piecewise covers the input
  defined at 16 of 16 points
  over all 16 points of the domain
> s2(x: i8 in -128..-1) = x*x
> range s2
range of s2 -> i8:
  -127..121   (44 distinct values)
  values: -127, -124, -119, -112..-111, -103, -95, -92, -87, -79, -71, -63, -60, -55, -47, -39, -31, -28, -23, -15, -7, 0..1, 4, 9, 16..17, ...
  fits in i8
  over all 128 points of the domain
> domain s2
domain of s2:
  x: i8, -128..-1
  C assumes x*x does not overflow i8  (signed overflow)
  defined at 128 of 128 points
  C undefined behaviour at 117 points, e.g. x=-128
  over all 128 points of the domain
> b0(x: u8 in 0..0) = x
